6: 
7: - **Units.** 21 through the band: 7 in grade 3 (units 1 to 7), 7 in grade 4 (units 8 to 14), 7 in grade 5 (units 15
8:   to 21). Six of them are sensitive units (6, 7, 13, 14, 20, 21), each removable. Each year is whole on its own: its
9:   own units, end-of-unit assessments, mid-year check and year-end check.
10: - **Days.** 90: 30 a year for three years. Grades 3 and 4 have 26 days in units and 4 course-level days (a mid-year
11:   check and its respond day, a year-end check and its respond day); grade 5 has 25 days in units and 5 course-level
12:   days (its year-end check has two respond days, below). One lesson a week, 30 minutes long, 30 weeks a year (weeks 1
13:   to 30 of the 32-week school year; weeks 31 and 32 hold core year-end checks in all three grades and no health), so
14:   the 30 minutes hold the opening scenario, the work, clean-up (active days name five minutes for it) and collecting
15:   sheets. The dose is the health approach page's default exactly (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, Pacing: K to 5
16:   about 30 sessions a year of 30 minutes, kept by Cory 2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-2). Days are weighed by load,
17:   not equal shares: a row that is one plain fact shares a day with its neighbor, a row that introduces a step card (a
18:   skill model) gets its own day, and every unit's last day is its end-of-unit assessment. Each unit's days include its
19:   end-of-unit assessment (no unit here runs over 25 days, so none has a mid-unit assessment). At one lesson a week a
20:   unit of four days runs four school weeks, which is what lets the grade 5 habit log (unit 15) run for several weeks
21:   inside its unit. These are course days: docs/courses/CALENDAR.md places them on school weeks. The national figure is
22:   higher: the 2007 National Health Education Standards (second edition, page 76) recommend at least 80 hours a year in
23:   grades 3 to 12; this course gives 15 hours a year. The course page names that figure and the gap so a school can add
24:   time (APPROACH.md, Pacing). A sensitive unit's days count as optional (STANDARD.md section 5 rule 6): a school that
25:   leaves one out uses its days to extend the non-sensitive unit named in its row. A school that meets health twice a
26:   week for half the year keeps the same order and checks; the scope and sequence says so in its short path.
27: - **Standard.** National Health Education Standards, 2024, SHAPE America: the 39 indicators of the 3-5 span
28:   (`NHES.S<n>.5.<n>`), each owned by one row (11 in grade 3, 14 in grade 4, 14 in grade 5) and placed in one unit
29:   here; ids and our own words only (data/standards/nhes-2024.txt), never the indicator text. Review tool: CDC's
30:   Health Education Curriculum Analysis Tool (HECAT), 2021 edition, keyed to the 2007 standards
31:   (docs/standards-notes/health-2007-to-2024.md); each unit names the healthy behavior outcomes it serves
32:   (`HECAT.<topic>.HBO<n>`), every day ties to one (bar criterion 1), and the units together cover the 3 to 5 column
33:   of the STANDARD.md section 4 map. Every year touches all eight standards, and every skill standard (2 to 8) is
34:   practiced in at least two topics in the band (STANDARD.md section 4), counted with the sensitive rows taken out so
35:   the claim holds in a school that leaves them out: standard 2 in personal health, mental health and tobacco; 3 in
36:   safety, personal health, alcohol and other drugs, and food; 4 in mental health, violence prevention, tobacco,
37:   safety, and alcohol and other drugs; 5 in safety, food and mental health; 6 in personal health and physical
38:   activity (units 8 and 15, through two row tags added in this pass, For the lead 3); 7 in personal health,
39:   physical activity and mental health; 8 in physical activity, violence prevention, personal health, tobacco,
40:   safety and food. The C3 column of the Units table holds, for health, the skill standard the unit teaches or
41:   practices and its step card (the model). Check set: each unit planner reads the HECAT 2021 module of its topic,
42:   3-5 knowledge and skill expectations, before planning (STANDARD.md section 3); the "Check set adds" column names
43:   what that read is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds, with the health lines of "What a
44:   health day never does" (APPROACH.md).
45: - **Base text.** None. Health is Curio Class's own: scenarios, step cards, short character cases, invented labels and
46:   ads, and family notes are written by Curio Class. Facts come from public health agencies (CDC, the federal food and
47:   drug agency's label pages, USDA's food guide pages, the national highway safety agency, the national poison help
48:   line), each with its source and year, checked by the health expert agent (below). No trade book, paid video or
49:   library visit.
50: - **The health unit shape.** Each unit names its behavior outcomes, three objectives (knowing, valuing, doing) and
51:   one skill with its steps (APPROACH.md). A skill is taught in the bar's five steps: why it matters, the steps, a
52:   model (the teacher says each step aloud on a scenario), practice on a short scenario, feedback. Grade 3 to 5 step
53:   cards are four or five short written steps. The teacher holds the model on the first fluency day, pairs hold the
54:   card on the second, the card is withdrawn on the first application day, and the end-of-unit assessment is taken
55:   without it. At one lesson a week a skill that returns gets its card back for one day, then goes without. Norms and
56:   pressure (bar criterion 4): each year states one plain-word norm on an Understanding day of a non-sensitive unit,
57:   in words with no figure below grade 6 (APPROACH.md, Kinds of day): grade 3 unit 2, "most people your age do not
58:   smoke or vape"; grade 4 unit 12, "most children your age have never tried a cigarette or a vape"; grade 5 unit 17,
59:   "most students your age do not drink alcohol or use drugs" (each from CDC youth and adult use pages for the nearest
60:   age group, source and year at the expert read). Pressure is met in the drink and tobacco ads (unit 2), the offered
61:   vape, dare and snack (unit 12), and a group's offer and an online stranger's request (unit 17).
62: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day"): Understanding (first teaching of a fact or idea, ending in a
63:   choice and a reason that uses a fact from the day), Fluency (practice only: the scripted pair rehearsals of a step
64:   card, a refusal line, an I-statement, an ask for help, a conflict script), Application (the skill on a new scenario,
65:   on a real class or school case, or on the student's own safe-list goal), Check (the end-of-unit assessment) and
66:   Respond (the course-level respond days). Fluency in this course is short: a five to eight minute pair rehearsal
67:   inside most days, and at most one whole Fluency day a unit.
68: - **End-of-unit evidence.** Every unit ends on a check day: a sitting with unseen short written scenarios (read aloud
69:   on request), on which each student writes or tells what the character would say or do using the steps, scored on
70:   the skill steps with a guide, plus one facts-in-use item per fact the unit lists and one valuing item (what would be
71:   a good choice for this character and why, scored on whether the reason uses a unit fact, never on which choice;
72:   APPROACH.md). In units that teach a spoken step (4, 10, 12, 18), the spoken step is heard from every student across
73:   the unit's last two days on a class record sheet, a third of the class on the second-to-last day and the rest on
74:   the check day while others write. Never a fact-recall test; no score on a day a topic is first taught. Twice a unit
75:   the student rates their own use of the steps on a self-check card and names one next step (bar criterion 8). The
76:   teacher marks met, partly or not yet. Where an owned indicator asks the student to do something, the evidence
77:   includes the student doing it at school: NHES.S1.5.4 in unit 9 (each student shows one taught routine step by step,
78:   on a teacher record), NHES.S6.5.5 and NHES.S7.5.2 in unit 15 (a habit log kept at school for the unit's four weeks,
79:   with a teacher record of each student carrying out the habit in ordinary school routines and one check-in with a
80:   helper), and NHES.S8.5.4 in unit 19 (a message delivered to a real audience). These are scored on the steps and
81:   the student's telling, never on health, home habits or whether a goal worked. A character's log or plan shows
82:   understanding only and never stands alone for a doing indicator. Unit 19 is a product unit: its draft day and its
83:   written feedback and revision day come before the scored delivery and are counted in its days.
84: - **Readiness and retrieval.** No readiness check in this course. Every row grade 3 assumes is a grade 2 row of
85:   Health, grades K to 2 taught the year before, every row grade 4 assumes is a grade 3 row of this course taught the
86:   year before, and every row grade 5 assumes is a grade 4 row taught the year before, so each opens as a short
87:   retrieval task inside a day of the unit that needs it (sequence-write.md rule 2), with the earlier unit named in the
88:   unit's row below. The count starts from the grade of the year being taught; there are no routes. A student new to
89:   Curio Class health shows any gap in the retrieval task, and the teacher reteaches that step on the card that day.
90: - **Sensitive units and the leave-out rule.** STANDARD.md section 5: sensitive content lives only in its own units
91:   (body safety: units 6, 13, 20; respect, growing up and consent: units 7, 14, 21); no skill lives only in a
92:   sensitive unit (each indicator a sensitive row touches is owned by a non-sensitive row); getting help is taught in a
93:   non-sensitive unit every year (units 3, 10, 16 and 17); no non-sensitive unit assumes a sensitive one; each
94:   sensitive unit has a leave-out note on its first page and a longer family note (English and Spanish) sent home two
95:   weeks before. Each year's sensitive units sit after its non-sensitive units and right before the year-end check, and
96:   no course-level check samples a sensitive unit, so leaving one out changes no check. In sensitive units rehearsal
97:   uses written scripts of the refusal or telling line only; the teacher or a card plays any pressure; no student plays
98:   a person who pressures, touches or threatens (APPROACH.md). Growing-up units (14, 21) name who is affected by leaving
99:   them out (children whose bodies change early) and the school offers a private route.
100: - **Reading level.** Students read at most a short scenario alone (a few sentences at the grade's level); longer
101:   cases, labels and source pages are read aloud or read together (APPROACH.md, Supports). Each unit teaches six to
102:   ten health words in depth with a sentence frame for each skill step ("I feel ___ when ___ because ___. Please
103:   ___."; "No thanks. I'm going to ___."). The right idea said in another language or shown by pointing is a language
104:   gap, not a content gap. No student must speak aloud: every rehearsal and check item can be done in writing, by
105:   pointing at a card or with an adult.
106: - **Family notes.** Every unit sends a home task (a talk with a trusted adult the student chooses; a school adult
107:   counts), written in English and Spanish when the unit is finished. No task asks a student about their own home,
108:   food, body, feelings, conditions or family; scenarios are about characters, and personal plans stay with the
109:   student unless they choose to share.
110: - **Devices.** None needed; no owned code needs technology. NHES.S1.5.5, S3.5.3 and S3.5.4 (finding and judging
111:   health information) are met on printed source pages, invented labels and flyers and a question to the school nurse;
112:   where a unit plan adds a teacher-chosen trusted website, the teacher shows it on one class screen and the same page
113:   is printed for the paper path. Any Curio Class practice tool a unit plan adds (a source-check sort, a step-card
114:   practice) runs on one teacher screen, collects no health information and has a paper path with the same items.
115: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on paper scenario cards, step cards, printed labels and logs; any electronic tool has
116:   the same task on paper beside it, and no student account is ever made. Online safety (units 3 and 17) is taught on
117:   printed invented screens and messages, with no app or site named.
118: - **Safety.** Hands-on days and their controls are named in the last column of the Units table. Guides the safety
119:   read uses: CDC, "Clean Hands" handwashing pages (https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/); the national highway traffic
120:   safety agency's bicycle safety pages (https://www.nhtsa.gov/road-safety/bicycle-safety); the federal Poison Help
121:   pages (https://poisonhelp.hrsa.gov/); CDC's school asthma and food allergy pages
122:   (https://www.cdc.gov/school-health-conditions/); the school's own food, allergy, emergency and active-play rules.
123:   Links named from memory, opened at the safety and expert reads. No real medicine, inhaler, injector, alcohol,
124:   cigarette, vape or lighter is ever brought to class; pictures and invented labels only. Drink labels are printed or
125:   on clean empty containers the teacher brings; no tasting.
126: - **Expert read (bar criterion 7).** The Curio Class health expert agent reads every unit plan and day for medical
127:   and scientific accuracy against current public health sources and records the date (STANDARD.md section 6). The
128:   course page may say figures were last checked against their sources on a date, and that a school's health staff
129:   should read it too.
130: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
131:   no outside reader is planned (settled 2026-11-14), and the safety and answer-key reads are agent reads too. Units
132:   that need the agent to study named sources first: units 6, 13 and 20 (body safety: children who have been harmed
133:   and their families; the federal Child Welfare Information Gateway pages on preventing child sexual abuse (the exact
134:   page named at the sensitivity read; the Prevention page, https://www.childwelfare.gov/topics/preventing/, is the way
135:   in), CDC's child abuse prevention pages and a national child traumatic stress network's guidance for teachers);
136:   units 7, 14 and 21 (respect, growing up and consent: families of every structure, faith and culture, including
137:   same-sex parents, kinship and foster families; children of every gender and who they like; children whose bodies
138:   change early; children living with HIV or with a family member who does; the HECAT 2021 Sexual Health module 3-5
139:   expectations, a national pediatrics association's puberty pages for families, and CDC's HIV basics pages); units 2,
140:   15 and 19 (food and drinks: children with too little food at home, cultural foods, and the risk of early body and
141:   weight talk; USDA's food guide pages for families and a national eating disorders association's guidance for
142:   schools); unit 9 (long-term conditions: children who live with asthma, an allergy or diabetes; CDC's school health
143:   conditions pages); units 3, 10 and 17 (safety, emergencies, medicine and alcohol: children who have lived through
144:   an injury or an emergency, or live with someone who smokes, drinks or uses drugs; no blame on a family member);
145:   units 4, 12 and 18 (bullying, limits and conflict: children who are bullied now). Every unit with hands-on or
146:   active work (1, 2, 8, 10, 15) also gets the safety agent's read against the guides above, and every check gets the
147:   answer-key agent's read.
148: - **Course-level checks.** Placed off the core check weeks (`frame.py calendar`): grade 3's core checks fall in weeks
149:   9, 12, 14, 16, 20, 23, 25, 28, 30, 31 and 32; grade 4's in 8, 10, 12, 16, 19, 21, 23, 27, 30, 31 and 32; grade 5's
150:   in 10, 11, 16, 17, 21, 22, 29, 30, 31 and 32. Health checks fall in week 15 (grade 3 and grade 5 mid-year), week 14

