| 1 | K | Healthy me. *What does a healthy body and mind need every day?* | 5 | k-phw-wellness-parts; k-phw-body-names; k-phw-hand-washing; k-phw-sleep-rest | NHES.S1.2.2; NHES.S1.2.4; NHES.S7.2.1 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7): hand washing in five pictured steps (wet, soap, scrub while you sing a short song twice, about 20 seconds, rinse, dry; CDC "Clean Hands") | Day 1: healthy has parts (body, mind, friends), sorted from pictured actions (NHES.S1.2.2). Day 2: hand washing done for real at the sink with its moments (before eating, after the toilet, after coughing), and why: washing keeps me and the people I touch from getting sick, a habit that helps me and others (NHES.S1.2.4, NHES.S7.2.1). Day 3 (week 3): body names, outside and main inside parts (heart, lungs, stomach, brain) and the private part names said once, with the idea of private (~NHES.S1.2.2 practice); words only, nothing about what parts do. Day 4: sleep and a calm bedtime routine as a picture list (~NHES.S7.2.1, ~NHES.S1.2.4 practice). Day 5: end-of-unit check: unseen pictured cards: order the washing steps, pick the moment to wash and say who it protects, sort an action under body, mind or friends; valuing item on a character who skips washing. Out: germ science (grade 1), how long a child sleeps. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2, HBO5. Tools: a picture sort (paper; optional one-screen version). Curio Class writes: wellness sort cards, step card, a bedtime picture list, the body-names script and family note. Cost (class of 30, provisional): $3 printing; soap and paper towels from school stock | First because it names the parts of health the whole band sorts by, and hand washing is a daily school habit worth starting in week 2. Body names sit on day 3 (week 3) so the family note sent in week 1 gives two weeks for preview and opt-out, and they come before unit 5, which needs them. No earlier course; no readiness check | Candidate: HECAT PHW module PreK-2 read may add a tooth-brushing or dental-care expectation; add it inside day 4 if it fits, or list it under For the lead | Body names: person read before publishing (later.csv L89); family note in week 1 with the words first, no pictures, preview and a quiet opt-out of that part; teacher answers a question in one calm sentence and moves on; if a child says anything about being touched or hurt, listen, do not promise secrecy, do not question, follow the school's duty to report. Hand washing: wipe sink spills at once (slips), fragrance-free soap, five minutes of the period kept for washing and drying; a child without soap at home is never singled out. Sleep: never ask about a child's bedtime. Agent reads: sensitivity (sources named in the brief); safety (sink) |
| 2 | K | Food, water and play. *What helps my body grow and feel strong?* | 6 | k-fn-foods-for-growing; k-fn-water-first; k-pa-move-every-day; k-phw-things-that-help; k-phw-pick-a-habit | NHES.S2.2.1; NHES.S6.2.1 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): the influence check introduced (helps or hurts, say one why); setting goals (standard 6): goal-plan step 1, pick one habit | Foods that help us grow and foods to have less often, named by color, never good or bad; water as the drink bodies need most (~NHES.S1.2.4 and ~NHES.S7.2.1 practice, owned in unit 1); playing hard makes the heart beat faster, with a water break (health teaches why and the habit, PE teaches the moving). Then things around us that help or hurt (sleep, washing, play, too much sun, foods), sorted with one why: the first influence check (NHES.S2.2.1, ~NHES.S2.2.1 practice in the food rows). Then pick one healthy habit to try from a short safe list of what the class has learned (washing, water, a sleep routine, time outside) and draw it (NHES.S6.2.1). End-of-unit check on day 6: unseen pictured cards sorted helps or hurts with one why; pick a habit for a character and say why it helps. Out: food groups by name and labels (grade 3), any amount, weight or calorie. Serves HECAT.FN.HBO1, HBO2, HBO3, HBO6, HBO7; PA.HBO1, HBO4; PHW.HBO1, HBO5. Tools: helps-or-hurts picture sort (paper first). Curio Class writes: food and drink picture cards, a pictured plate, the habit card. Cost: $3 printing; optional tasting of two fruits or vegetables, $15 for 30 children | After unit 1 because the influence sort uses unit 1's sleep and washing as its first "helps", and the habit pick draws on units 1 and 2. Second in the year because food and play are concrete and positive before feelings and safety. Ends before the mid-year check in week 12 | Candidate: HECAT FN and PA modules PreK-2 read may add a breakfast or "listen to hunger and fullness" expectation; keep it to one sentence inside the food day | Food: never ask what a child eats or drinks at home; foods and budgets vary; no good or bad foods, no weighing, no calories. Tasting is optional, under the school's food and allergy rules, with allergies checked first; otherwise pictures. Active bursts: clear space, a water break, a child who cannot run takes part by moving arms or clapping. Goals: never about weight, body or food limits; a character's goal is always an option. Agent reads: sensitivity (food sources named in the brief); safety (tasting, active play) |
| 3 | K | Feelings and friends. *What can I do with a big feeling, and how do I show I am listening?* | 5 | k-meh-feeling-words; k-meh-calm-body; k-meh-good-listening; k-v-kind-words-and-hands | NHES.S4.2.1; NHES.S4.2.2; NHES.S4.2.5 | Communicating (standard 4): the I-statement and the listening steps introduced | Naming a feeling and a need with the frame "I feel ___ when ___. I need ___." (NHES.S4.2.1), the I-statement words this course keeps through grade 2; calm-down tools practiced when calm on the one calm-down card shared with SEL: SEL kindergarten unit 2's "stop, breathe" with the slow balloon breath, retrieved here, and the child chooses when to use it (~NHES.S4.2.1 practice); listening in four pictured steps with a partner who is different from me, practiced in two places, the class circle and the lunch table or playground (NHES.S4.2.2); kind words and gentle hands, the first step on bullying: unkind acts hurt and an adult can help (NHES.S4.2.5). End evidence: the I-statement said for a character heard from every child across days 4 and 5; day 5 check on unseen pictured scenes (pick the kind act, pick a calm tool for a character). Out: bullying roles (grade 3). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO1, HBO2, HBO4, HBO5, HBO7; V.HBO1, HBO3. Tools: feeling-face cards. Curio Class writes: face cards, step cards, short kind and unkind scenes with characters varied in family, ability and culture. Cost: $3 printing | After the mid-year check, when the class knows its routines; before unit 4 because asking a helper uses the I-statement and listening steps taught here. SEL kindergarten unit 2 (weeks 5 to 10) teaches the feeling faces and the "stop, breathe" card first (Cross-subject), so this unit opens them as retrieval and spends its first day on the I-statement frame | Candidate: HECAT MEH module PreK-2 read may add "show care for others' feelings"; it fits inside the kind-words day | No child is asked about their own feelings at home; feelings are a character's. A calm-down tool is never used as a punishment. A child who does not speak points to a face card. Agent reads: sensitivity |
| 4 | K | Helpers and choices. *Who helps keep me safe, and what do I do when I have a choice?* | 6 | k-s-trusted-helpers; k-s-notice-a-choice; k-s-safety-gear-and-cars; k-aod-medicine-safety; k-t-smoke-and-vapor | NHES.S3.2.1; NHES.S5.2.1 | Accessing help (standard 3): help-steps step 1, know your helpers; deciding (standard 5): decision-steps step 1, notice a choice | What makes a trusted adult: one who helps keep you safe and listens; children sort pictured adults at home, school and nearby by those two signs, then choose some of their own on a hand drawing (families vary; a school adult always counts) (NHES.S3.2.1). SEL kindergarten unit 1 has named the school helpers by picture; this unit adds home and nearby helpers and the two signs. One scripted ask to a helper, "Can you help me?", rehearsed in pairs (~NHES.S8.2.1 practice, owned in grade 1 unit 7). Notice a choice: stop and say "I have a choice" (NHES.S5.2.1). Seat belts, car seats and helmets lower the chance of getting hurt (~NHES.S1.2.3, owned in grade 1 unit 6); medicine only from a trusted grown-up, pills and cleaners left alone; smoke and vapor are not good for lungs: stay away and tell, with the norm said plainly, most grown-ups do not smoke or vape (both ~NHES.S1.2.3). End-of-unit check on day 6: unseen pictured scenes: point to a helper for each and point to the sign card that shows why they are trusted, spot the choice, pick "leave it and tell". Out: any drug lesson; car-seat fitting. Serves HECAT.S.HBO1, HBO3, HBO4, HBO5, HBO7; AOD.HBO1; T.HBO1, HBO2. Tools: helper hand drawing, leave-it-and-tell picture sort. Curio Class writes: helper cards, choice scenes, a pictured "leave it and tell" card. Cost: $3 printing; one child's helmet and a doll, borrowed (or $15 for a helmet) | The getting-help unit every year needs, in a non-sensitive place, so unit 5 can be left out. After unit 3 because naming helpers uses the I-statement for what to say. Gear, medicine and smoke share the day plan of "notice, leave it, tell": one action, three settings | Candidate: HECAT S module PreK-2 read may add street-crossing or "know my address" expectations; one scene each inside the choice day | Helmets fitted on a doll or a partner's own school helmet only, never shared between heads. Pictures only for medicines, cleaners, cigarettes and vapes. A child who lives with someone who smokes is never made to feel blame for that person. Agent reads: sensitivity (safety sources named in the brief); safety |
| 5 | K | My body is mine (sensitive, removable). *What do I do if a touch does not feel safe?* | 2 | k-v-body-safety | | Accessing help and communicating: help-steps practiced (no new skill) | Body safety in simple words: my body is mine; some touches are safe and some are not; secrets about touch are never kept; tell a trusted adult until one helps. Practices NHES.S3.2.1 (owned in unit 4) and begins NHES.S4.2.3 (owned in grade 1 unit 7); owns no indicator. Day 1 understanding; day 2 written-script practice of the telling line with a card, then the end-of-unit check: unseen pictured scenes, point to "tell" and to a helper. Leave-out note: the course still teaches trusted helpers and the help steps in unit 4; leaving it out removes HECAT.V.HBO8 for the year. Serves HECAT.V.HBO8. Curio Class writes: leave-out note, longer family note (English and Spanish), the scripts. Cost: $2 printing | After unit 4 (helpers, help-steps) and unit 1 (correct names). Last before the year-end check so the check never depends on it; if left out, its two days extend unit 4 | Candidate: HECAT V module PreK-2 read may add "surprises are okay, secrets about touch are not"; already in day 1 | Sensitive. Family note two weeks before, with the outline and how to opt out through the school (at home the parent decides). No child is asked about their own experience; no role play of a person who touches; a card plays any pressure. If a child tells you something: listen, do not promise to keep it secret, follow the school's duty to report, and tell the child who the next adult is; a parent teaching at home is pointed to the national child abuse hotline by purpose. Agent reads: sensitivity (sources named in the brief) |
| 6 | 1 | Habits that keep germs away. *How do I keep germs from spreading, and what am I already good at?* | 4 | g1-phw-my-strengths; g1-phw-stop-germs; g1-phw-eyes-ears-sun; g1-phw-daily-habits | NHES.S1.2.1; NHES.S1.2.3; NHES.S7.2.2 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7) for me and for others; health information (standard 1) | Day 1: strengths that keep me healthy (washing, trying foods, asking for help, being kind), one for body, mind and friends, never body traits (NHES.S1.2.1); the daily-habits picture chart starts, kept for a week by the child's own choice and never scored. Doing (NHES.S7.2.2): across the unit the teacher keeps a class record of each child carrying out the taught routines at school (washing before snack, covering a cough into the elbow, not sharing a cup) after the model and feedback, marked met, partly or not yet on the steps. Day 2: germs are tiny living things that spread from person to person; wash, cough into the elbow, stay home when sick, do not share cups (NHES.S1.2.3); a washable-paint "germ" handshake shown by the teacher; the cough-and-wash card is modeled by the teacher and practiced in pairs. Day 3: protecting eyes, ears and skin (hats, shade, sunglasses, sunscreen put on by an adult, turning sound down) (~NHES.S1.2.3 practice), and the norm said from the class's own routine record ("most of our class covered a cough this week"), never naming a child. Day 4: end-of-unit check: unseen pictured scenes, pick the germ-stopping action and say why; match a body part to its protection; the routine record is carried into the unit judgment for NHES.S7.2.2. Out: how germs make people sick, any illness story. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2, HBO3, HBO4, HBO5. Curio Class writes: strength cards, a habits picture chart, a cough-and-wash step card. Cost: $4 (printing, washable paint) | First in grade 1 because germ habits build straight on kindergarten unit 1 and are used every school day. Retrieval tasks open day 1 (k-phw-wellness-parts) and day 2 (k-phw-hand-washing, k-phw-sleep-rest), all kindergarten unit 1, the year before; day 3 retrieves outside body names only (kindergarten unit 1) | Candidate: HECAT PHW module PreK-2 read may add hearing and vision checks; one sentence inside day 3 | No blame for who gets sick; no illness stories. Washable paint only, with clean-up time; no sunscreen product handled by children. The habits chart stays with the child. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (paint, hand washing) |
| 7 | 1 | Asking for help. *When something feels wrong, who do I tell and what do I say?* | 6 | g1-s-ask-for-help; g1-meh-tell-a-helper; g1-s-unsafe-feels-wrong; g1-s-choose-and-ask; g1-aod-medicine-helpers | NHES.S3.2.2; NHES.S8.2.1; NHES.S4.2.3; NHES.S5.2.2 | Accessing help (standard 3), communicating (standard 4) and advocacy (standard 8): help-steps (say who you need, what happened, that it matters) with the I-statement and listening steps | When and how to ask for help: who, what happened, that it is important, practiced in pairs with a card (NHES.S3.2.2); asking a trusted adult for help with a worry that will not go away and listening to what they say: "I feel ___ and I need help." (NHES.S8.2.1; the worry scene also practices the help steps, ~NHES.S3.2.2, so standard 3 is practiced in mental health as well as safety); what to say or do when something feels wrong or unsafe: say stop, move away, tell, keep telling (scenes: a rough game, a stranger asking for something, a scary place, a found vape or pills) (NHES.S4.2.3); choices I make alone and choices that need a grown-up (NHES.S5.2.2); the people who give medicine safely and why we ask first (~NHES.S1.2.3 practice, owned in unit 6). End evidence: the help request said for a character heard from every child across days 5 and 6; day 6 check on unseen pictured scenes. Out: emergencies and calling for help (grade 2). Serves HECAT.S.HBO4, HBO5, HBO6, HBO7; MEH.HBO4; V.HBO7; AOD.HBO1. Curio Class writes: help-step card, scripted scenes with an adult playing any pressure, a choose-alone-or-ask sort. Cost: $3 | The getting-help unit for grade 1 in a non-sensitive place, so units 10 and 11 can be left out. Before the mid-year check because units 9 and 10 use its steps. Retrieval opens it: k-s-trusted-helpers (kindergarten unit 4) on day 1, k-meh-feeling-words and k-meh-good-listening (kindergarten unit 3) on day 2, k-s-notice-a-choice, k-t-smoke-and-vapor and k-aod-medicine-safety (kindergarten unit 4) on day 4, all the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH and S module PreK-2 reads may add "tell an adult if someone online asks for something"; one scene on day 3 | No child is asked about their own worries or family; scenes are characters'. Stranger scenes stay calm, with no kidnapping detail. Never test knowledge of home medicines. Agent reads: sensitivity (safety sources named in the brief) |
| 8 | 1 | What shapes my health. *What around me helps me be healthy, and how do I choose a snack?* | 5 | g1-phw-surroundings; g1-phw-how-things-touch-me; g1-fn-plate-and-water; g1-fn-snack-options | NHES.S1.2.6; NHES.S2.2.2; NHES.S5.2.3 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): the influence check (name it, say how it touches me); deciding (standard 5): decision steps 2 and 3 | Where we live and learn affects health (clean water, safe play places, crowded or quiet rooms), drawn as a healthy place of the child's choice, never their own home, and how a clean, safe shared place (clean water, clean air, a safe playground) helps everyone who uses it, not only one child (NHES.S1.2.6, personal and community health); how things touch my own health and feelings (a late night makes me tired) (NHES.S2.2.2); a pictured plate with a range of foods and a water cup, and what a snack ad wants (~NHES.S2.2.2 practice); two or three snack options and what could happen with each (NHES.S5.2.3). End-of-unit check on day 5: unseen pictured cards, name an influence and how it touches a character; say how a shared place helps the people who use it; give two options and a result for a character's snack. Out: food groups by name (grade 3). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO5; FN.HBO1, HBO2, HBO3, HBO6, HBO7. Curio Class writes: place pictures, a pictured plate, two snack ads invented for practice. Cost: $3 | After the mid-year check, using unit 7's choose-and-ask as decision step 1 to 2. Retrieval: k-phw-things-that-help, k-fn-foods-for-growing and k-fn-water-first (kindergarten unit 2), and k-s-notice-a-choice (kindergarten unit 4), the year before | Candidate: HECAT FN module PreK-2 read may add "eat breakfast"; one scene inside the snack day | Homes differ: children describe school and a place they choose, never their own home. Snack options all stay foods that help or foods for sometimes; no scolding. Ads are invented for practice, with no product names. Agent reads: sensitivity (food sources named in the brief) |
| 9 | 1 | Playing safe together, and a goal to try. *How can playtime stay safe and fair, and what healthy thing will I work on?* | 5 | g1-pa-play-safe; g1-v-settle-without-hurting; g1-s-help-others-stay-safe; g1-phw-name-a-goal; g1-phw-goal-helpers-and-steps | NHES.S8.2.2; NHES.S6.2.2; NHES.S6.2.3; NHES.S6.2.4 | Advocacy (standard 8): helping others choose safely; setting goals (standard 6): goal-plan with helpers and steps | Active play stays safe with space, rules, water and stopping when hurt; the helmet rule carried (~NHES.S7.2.2 practice, owned in unit 6); small playground problems settled with words, taking turns or getting an adult: "Stop, I don't like that" (~NHES.S4.2.3 practice, owned in unit 7); ways to help others make safe choices (remind a friend to buckle up, tell an adult when a friend is hurt) (NHES.S8.2.2). Then a goal in the frame "I will ___" from the safe list and why it helps (NHES.S6.2.2), who can help and two or three steps in order, drawn (NHES.S6.2.3, NHES.S6.2.4). End-of-unit check on day 5: the child's goal plan shown (goal, helper, steps), or a character's, scored on the goal-plan steps; one unseen scene on helping a friend. Out: acting on and looking back on the goal (grade 2). Serves HECAT.PA.HBO1, HBO4, HBO6; V.HBO1, HBO3, HBO7; S.HBO8; PHW.HBO1, HBO2. Curio Class writes: safe-play rule cards, goal frame sheet. Cost: $3; cones from PE stock | Play-safe and settle share a day plan (a game gets rough), then advocacy, then the goal, last of the grade 1 non-sensitive units, so the plan goes home with ten weeks of the school year left to try it. Uses help-steps from unit 7. Retrieval: k-pa-move-every-day and k-phw-pick-a-habit (kindergarten unit 2), k-s-safety-gear-and-cars and k-s-trusted-helpers (kindergarten unit 4), k-v-kind-words-and-hands (kindergarten unit 3), the year before | Candidate: HECAT PA module PreK-2 read may add "warm up before play"; one line inside day 1 | Active play: clear space, water, stop when hurt; a child who cannot run takes part in another way. Goals from the safe list only; a character's goal is always an option; the plan stays with the child. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (active play) |
