288 | 9 | 4 | Pulse, agility and the fitness chart. *What does my pulse tell me about how hard I am working, and which activities build speed, balance and quick reactions?* | 10 | g4-pulse-body; g4-skill-fitness | SHAPE.S2.5.12; SHAPE.S2.5.14; SHAPE.S2.5.10 | Cues: "two fingers, wrist or neck, count to ten"; the fitness-parts chart. Settings: rest, warm-up and circuit stations of skill-fitness tasks | Find the pulse at the wrist or neck, count it for ten seconds at rest and after activity, and name activities that raise the heart rate (S2.5.12); notice and describe how activity changes the body: breath, warmth, sweat, a faster beat (S2.5.14); describe and show activities that build the skill-related fitness parts: agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time and speed, added to the fitness-parts chart (S2.5.10). The heart pumps blood to the working muscles, said in one line (science grade 4 teaches the body's structures later this year, Cross-subject). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 4's fitness parts and benefits and unit 1's warm-up. Out: beats per minute and target zones (grade 6). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: shows one activity for a named skill-related part at a station, with a warm-up led for a partner as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: finds a pulse and says what changed after the circuit; matches an activity to a skill-related part; conduct: waits for a clear station and helps a partner count, S3.5.3 review), 1 respond day. Tools: fitness-parts chart (unit 4's, extended), station cards (ours) | First in grade 4 because every later grade 4 warm-up uses the pulse and every game unit names the skill-related parts it builds; it reopens the year with grade 3's fitness chart through retrieval | Candidate: a private before-and-after pulse card the child keeps | Pulse counts private and never compared or posted; a child who cannot find a pulse uses a hand on the chest; no fitness scores. Sensitivity read sources: the national heart institute's page on the heart and physical activity. Cost: $0 (cones, ropes and balls reused) |
296 | 17 | 5 | My fitness plan. *How can how often, how hard, how long and what kind help me reach a fitness goal, and what can technology tell me about it?* | 14 | g5-fitt; g5-goal; g5-tech | SHAPE.S2.5.9; SHAPE.S4.5.6; SHAPE.S2.5.8; SHAPE.S2.5.16 | Cues: the FITT plan card; "baseline, try, check, chart". Settings: fitness circuit stations of the band's skills, the child's own goal station | Describe how frequency, intensity, time and type improve a skill or fitness, and change one part of a plan (S2.5.9; fitt-plan introduced); set a fitness goal (S2.5.8) and a visible goal for a longer stretch of time, about four to six weeks: a baseline on day 3, a check in each week's warm-up and a bar graph of the child's own counts, kept privately (S4.5.6); name technology that can help meet activity goals, a step counter, a timer, a skill video, and what each can and cannot show (S2.5.16). On the 2.5-a-week dose the goal runs from the baseline on day 3 to the check days 12 and 13, about four weeks; day 14 responds to the check. Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 9's pulse and skill-related parts and unit 14's strengths. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: the child's own goal station against their baseline, marked for the cue, never the count; knowledge tasks: changes one FITT part in a plan and says why; a goal with a baseline and a weekly check; one tool and one limit; conduct: keeps their record private and helps a station partner count, S3.5.3 review), 1 respond day. Tools: FITT plan card, goal card and bar graph sheet (ours); the teacher's watch | First in grade 5 so the goal's weeks run while the year is fresh and its checks sit in the warm-ups; FITT needs grade 4's fitness chart and pulse, and the goal uses the FITT card, so they share one unit | Candidate: a family-shared home activity added to the FITT card | Fitness data stays with the child: no comparing, ranking, posting or grade; the check marks the cue and the goal steps, never the count. No weighing or body measures. Devices are never required (Devices). Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' children's page. Cost: $5 (printing) |
299 | 20 | 5 | Fuel for moving. *How does what I eat and drink change how I feel and play?* | 6 | g5-food-drink | SHAPE.S2.5.17 | Cues: "water before, during, after". Settings: an active circuit with a water break, a picture sort between stations | Explain how what is eaten and drunk affects activity: water before, during and after, energy foods before long activity, a snack to recover (S2.5.17). Readiness check on g2-foods-energy (K to 2 unit 18, grade 2) on day 36; the effort faces and unit 9's body changes open as a retrieval task. Health grades 3 to 5 teaches the food groups and the everyday drinks (Cross-subject): PE teaches only food and water for activity, taught inside a moving circuit. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: a three-station circuit with the warm-up and cool-down from unit 1, its cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge task: picks a drink and a snack for before and after a long game from picture cards and says why; conduct: takes the water break and helps set the stations back, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day | Before the mid-year check so the check can sample it; it follows the FITT and game units because long activity is now the setting; six days because it is one knowledge row taught inside a circuit that moves the whole time | Candidate: a private "what helps me play" card for the family note | Food: foods a class commonly sees, never good or bad, never about body size or weight; allergies respected; no calorie or diet talk. Sensitivity read sources: the federal dietary guidelines' children's pages. Water break and shade on warm days. Cost: $3 (printing) |
