156 | 1 | Poems and becoming a writer. "How does reading poems help someone become a writer?" | 38 | e4-evidence-inference; e4-theme-summary; e4-character-depth; e4-lit-word-meaning; e4-poetry-drama; e4-narration-pov; e4-reading-range; e4-long-words; e4-fluency; e4-conventions; e4-writing-tasks; e4-discussion; e4-presenting; e4-informative; e4-writing-process; e4-grammar | RL.4.1; RI.4.1; W.4.9; RL.4.2; RL.4.3; RL.4.4; L.4.5; RL.4.5; RL.4.7; RL.4.6; RL.4.10; RI.4.10; RF.4.3; RF.4.4; L.4.2; W.4.4; W.4.10; SL.4.1; SL.4.4; SL.4.5; W.4.2; W.4.5; W.4.6; L.4.1 | Not used (ELA) | Anchors: an original Curio Class verse novel read whole (a present-day boy who thinks poems are not for him writes a year of poem-journal entries for his teacher; about 8,000 to 12,000 words, measure below the band, meaning above it); public-domain poems the boy reads (Robert Frost and William Carlos Williams from 1923, Langston Hughes from his 1926 book, Emily Dickinson, Christina Rossetti, leaving out her "Who Has Seen the Wind?", which grade 6 reads), each with a short original poet biography (measure target about 740 to 950); one short prose story told beside a verse retelling of the same moment, and a short drama scene from the same story. Referring to details when explaining what a text says and when inferring, in talk and in writing on literary texts (RL.4.1, RI.4.1, W.4.9a; 9b, informational texts, is continued in unit 2); a theme of a poem or of the verse novel from its details, and a summary (RL.4.2); the boy's change across the year described in depth (RL.4.3); similes and metaphors explained in context, idioms and proverbs, words linked to antonyms and near-synonyms (RL.4.4, L.4.5a to c; allusions to myth are taught in unit 3 with the myths); what makes a poem a poem (verse, rhythm, meter, line), drama's parts on the short scene, and how prose differs (RL.4.5); the prose story set beside a recorded reading of it and the drama scene beside a staged reading, students naming where the presentation follows particular descriptions or directions in the text (RL.4.7; the poem recordings are practice, the unit 3 play review); first-person narration of the verse novel against third-person poems and the prose story (RL.4.6). Independent reading launched in the 4 to 5 band (RL.4.10, RI.4.10). Writing: daily exit tickets and a four-paragraph informative essay on what inspires a poet, with introduction, body paragraphs grouped by aspect, facts, details and quotations from the biographies and poems, linking words, domain words and a conclusion (W.4.2a to e; the essay-structure model introduced), quotations punctuated (L.4.2b), with the rhetorical-situation model introduced (who reads it, why, what form) (W.4.4, W.4.10); the essay planned, critiqued against look-fors, revised and edited with peer and teacher feedback (W.4.5; using technology to produce and publish, W.4.6, is continued in unit 2 with one typed page and an online exchange); fragments and run-ons found and fixed in the essay (L.4.1f; modals L.4.1c and adjective order L.4.1d are continued in unit 2, progressive tenses 1b, prepositional phrases 1e and confused words 1g in unit 3, relative pronouns and adverbs 1a in unit 4); an original poem. Talk: discussion with roles (SL.4.1a to d); a poem presentation, the student's own poem read aloud and its inspiration explained, organized around a theme with a visual (SL.4.4, SL.4.5). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.4.3a); fluency on poems (RF.4.4a to c); spelling on the same patterns with reference checks (L.4.2d) and capitals (L.4.2a); the compound-sentence comma (2c) comes in unit 3. Diagnostic retrieval: the fall oral reading screen, grade 3 point of view, the discussion routine and sentences (simple, compound, complex). The unit's word list Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.4.1e, L.4.1g, L.4.2c (unit 3); W.4.9b (unit 2, unit 3). | The base text's module 1: short whole poems are the right first texts for close reading and fluency, the verse novel is a long text read whole at the start of the year, and the rhetorical-situation model, the discussion roles and the daily writing every later unit uses start here. The base text teaches and assesses the four-paragraph informative essay, its revision, fragments and run-ons and quotation punctuation in its unit 2 (W.4.2, W.4.5, L.4.1f, L.4.2b), so e4-informative, e4-writing-process and e4-grammar sit here. One day added for the start-of-year screens | Candidate: rubric 2a (a long text read whole), 1t and 1u (fluency practice with checks), 2e (poets of different eras and backgrounds), 2q (planning, critique and revision taught) | The verse novel touches the death of a pet (gentle, previewed). Students' own poems are shared by choice; no student is asked to write about family loss. Poets' biographies note hard lives plainly without dwelling. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
