LEARNING: Three groups around one school improvement the class proposes and carries out (student voice, APPROACH "Kinds of day"), with a twelfth session as a second Application for the improvement work (the moved session, Days). Group 1, propose and start: the class proposes a school improvement to the principal or student council with reasons and evidence, each student weighing its time cost and carrying out a part (SM7); the first step planned for an invented case first (a lost-and-found box that overflows), then the class improvement started though it takes recess time, others recruited (SM6); a split group decision led, and following well when one's own idea loses (RS7). Group 2, keep everyone in: the team works under a deadline and solves a team problem when one member falls behind, without blame (RS4; it also teaches AASL.III.D.2, the group's learning as one's own job, placed in SEL by library); in a mixed team, a clash of habits resolved with respect using the three steps, the habits drawn from invented teammates' homes (when to talk, how a group decides: a vote, or talking until everyone agrees), the ways taken by the unit plan from a cited source (RS3); specific feedback to a teammate whose work was overlooked, even when the group praises someone else (SO2). Group 3, speak up and thank: an invented case where the student's own friend group laughs at a schoolmate's mistake at the class concert, the safest upstander option chosen and rehearsed with the I-statement while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act, and why it fits (RS9); thanks to someone who helped the group at a cost to themselves, naming the cost (SO5). Retrieval: the grade 4 rows (units 7, 9, 10 and 11) in the openings; health grade 5 unit 19's speak-up plan (weeks 20 to 23) is a real-audience message, and this unit's upstander option is for a friend group. End-of-unit evidence: the year-end course-level check after this unit is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks): an unseen scene page (the safest upstander option and why; credit for overlooked work; a team problem without blame) and a team problem solved from an unseen card, watched against the look-fors. Carry it out: "who did we miss?" at the end of group work. Family activity: thank someone for help that cost them, naming the cost. Tools: the goal steps card, the talk turn card, the I-statement frame, the upstander plan card, the proposal sheet. Curio Class writes the proposal sheet, the lost-and-found case, the teammates' habits from their source and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing and up to $10 for improvement supplies the class chooses.
WHY: After unit 16 because speaking up when it may cost standing with friends follows holding a no against a social cost; it gathers the year into one real project. Eight sub-skills, three groups and 12 sessions, taking the session grade 5 moved (Days), its Check and Respond being the year-end check. Social studies grade 5 unit 10 (informed action, course days 147 to 160) comes later; the computer science grade 5 team project is recorded under Cross-subject. Sessions 42 to 51 of the year, weeks 21 to 26; the year-end check is sessions 52 and 53.

